repository-code: "https://github.com/ameen-banjar/daim-os-self-healing-artifact"
abstract: >-
  Reproducibility artifact for "Event-Driven Link-Failure Recovery for a
  DAIM-OS Client Using OVSDB Notifications and Reactive Path Recomputation"
  (Paper 3 in the DAIM six-paper programme, prototype-stage, not yet
  submitted). Contains an event-driven repair agent (daim_link_agent.py)
  that subscribes to real OVSDB Interface.link_state notifications,
  recomputes an alternate path with breadth-first search over a declared
  topology graph, installs it through the existing DAIM-OS OVS adapter, and
  suppresses repeated repair processing on a flapping link via an
  IDLE/ACTIVE/HELD-DOWN hold-down state machine, keyed by physical edge
  rather than interface name since v0.3.0. An edge is only treated as
  recovered once every interface observing it independently confirms `up`
  (v0.4.0) -- a persistent per-interface state, not a single last-report-wins
  value per edge. A fresh agent process also correctly reads OVSDB's initial
  table snapshot at startup (v0.5.0), rather than only matching later
  "new"-actioned rows, so an interface already down when the agent starts is
  detected and routed around instead of silently assumed up; that snapshot
  is now also validated to be exactly "up" or "down" rather than treating
  any unrecognised value as implicitly up, and every fatal startup path
  cleans up the OVSDB monitor subprocess rather than leaking it (v0.6.0).
  Four real defects are documented in the history: a stale-state bug
  (v0.2.0, found via external manuscript review); a cross-interface
  hold-down gap (v0.3.0, found by the first live-network run of the
  flapping-link protocol against a real Mininet/OVS link -- OVSDB reports a
  link's two interfaces' transitions independently, and the pre-fix
  hold-down only covered the interface that triggered the repair); a
  last-report-wins edge-recovery gap (v0.4.0, found by code review); and a
  startup-state synchronization gap (v0.5.0, found by code review and
  confirmed against a live ovsdb-server -- the pre-fix agent produced 100%
  packet loss when a link was already down at startup, 0% after the fix). A
  fifth, separate bug in this artifact's own verification harness
  (stage3_startup_already_down.py's packet-loss check, a substring match
  that misclassified a 100%-loss run as loss-free) is also documented and
  fixed (v0.6.0), with its own regression test -- the underlying 100%/0%
  result was unaffected, since the figure-generation script parsed the
  numeric loss value independently, but the harness's own boolean field was
  wrong until this fix. v0.7.0 extends link_state validation to the ongoing
  event stream, not just the startup snapshot (decide_link_event() now
  returns a distinct invalid_link_state action instead of silently falling
  through to a no-op), and fixes a mislabelling bug in the flapping-link
  comparison figure: its "BFS/recompute calls" row previously counted every
  non-suppressed action including "recovered", which never calls BFS, so it
  reported 4-to-2 when the real count (repair/noop/repair_failed only) is
  1-to-1 for this specific live schedule -- the BFS-call-reduction claim is
  Figure 5's logic-level result, not this live figure's. v0.8.0 adds
  multi-OVS connection multiplexing and per-hop flow routing
  (REMOTE_ENDPOINTS, load_topology_config(), select()-based multiplexing of
  multiple ovsdb-client monitor connections in monitor_link_rows()), measured
  against a real two-VM testbed joined by a GRE tunnel: a remote-edge
  failure -- on switches the agent shares no local OVSDB connection with --
  was correctly detected and repaired across five repetitions. v0.9.0 fixes
  two issues found by external review of that same result: apply_flow()
  bypassed the DAIM-OS OVS adapter (daim_ovs_flow) for remote bridges and
  called ovs-ofctl directly, inconsistent with this paper's own adapter
  claim -- fixed by routing both local and remote bridges through the same
  adapter binary, confirmed empirically to already support a remote target
  with no C-code change; and install_path()/withdraw_path() discarded
  apply_flow()'s per-call success/failure, so a partially-failed
  installation could be logged as repair_installed -- fixed by a new
  execute_repair() that only reports success, and only advances
  current_path, when every flow-mod call actually succeeded, otherwise
  reporting a distinct repair_incomplete event. The n=5 measurement was
  re-run against the fixed code: still 5/5 clean, mean repair-action time
  191.48 ms (up from 185.75 ms pre-fix, the honest cost of the extra
  process-spawn hop the adapter path adds), independently bounded by the
  packet-loss gap in the concurrent probe traffic (an independent process
  in the agent's own VM, not a different host -- corrected from an earlier,
  imprecise description). v0.10.0 fixes a second review pass's two further
  findings, neither requiring new live-network data: execute_repair()'s own
  failure path was itself unsound, since withdraw_path(current_path) runs
  before install_path(), so a withdraw-succeeded/install-failed outcome
  means neither the old nor the new path is actually installed -- reporting
  the old path as still current (v0.9.0's behaviour) was its own false
  claim; current_path now becomes None on any partial failure, with
  withdraw_path(None) and a subsequent repair attempt from None both
  handled safely without crashing. Separately, bfs_path()/path_to_flows()
  hardcoded the literal host names "h1"/"h2" instead of the declared
  SOURCE/DEST/HOST_ATTACHMENT globals load_topology_config() already allows
  overriding; harmless for both topologies measured here (both use
  h1/h2) but would have raised KeyError for a differently-named-host
  deployment, so this is a prerequisite for future multi-topology work,
  fixed now. This same pass also corrected a real math error in the
  v0.9.0 report's own cross-check, which claimed the agent-reported
  repair-action time fell inside the independent ping-outage bound in
  every repetition -- false for repetition 5 by 4.69 ms, now stated
  correctly as 4-of-5 with an honest explanation (repair_end_ns marks
  control-plane completion, not necessarily the same instant as data-plane
  restoration). v0.11.0 fixes a third review pass's three further findings:
  execute_repair()'s current_path=None (v0.10.0) was not itself a
  guarantee of retry, since decide_link_event() never re-triggers a repair
  for an edge already in down_edges -- fixed with maybe_retry_repair(),
  called from main()'s periodic tick, retrying a degraded state until the
  underlying failure clears with no new OVSDB event required; the startup
  path had the identical false-success gap, fixed by
  execute_startup_install() mirroring execute_repair()'s contract; and
  withdraw_path() matched flow deletion far too broadly (a bare in_port
  match, confirmed empirically to delete an unrelated flow sharing only
  that port) -- fixed with OpenFlow cookie scoping (AGENT_COOKIE),
  entirely within daim_link_agent.py, no adapter C-code change. The
  cookie-scoping fix changes the wire-level flow-mod strings sent on every
  call, so the n=5 multi-OVS measurement was re-run again: still 5/5
  clean, mean repair-action time 203.54 ms (up from 191.48 ms
  pre-cookie-scoping). v0.12.0 closes four further evidence-gate items from
  a fifth review pass. AGENT_COOKIE, a single fixed constant correct only
  for a single-agent deployment, is replaced by _agent_cookie(), derived
  deterministically per (SOURCE, DEST) pair, so two agents protecting
  different pairs on a shared switch no longer collide. The repair-action
  time metric definition (manuscript Section 6.3) is corrected -- the timer
  starts inside execute_repair(), after decide_link_event()'s own BFS has
  already run, not from the start of BFS recomputation as previously
  stated; a wording fix, no numbers changed. monitor_link_rows() now
  attempts one reconnect on a dead ovsdb-client monitor stream, reconciling
  state via resync_from_reconnect() and triggering a real repair if the
  resync reveals the installed path no longer avoids every down edge --
  verified live by killing a real remote monitor connection and confirming
  both a clean reconnect and continued correct detection/repair afterward.
  install_path() gained a forwarding-consistency pre-check
  (_conflicting_flow_cookie(), a read-only ovs-ofctl dump-flows query
  outside the adapter): a foreign-cookie flow already occupying the exact
  match a new install would use is now rejected rather than silently
  overwritten, verified live against a real conflicting flow. Most
  substantially, execute_repair() now implements a two-phase prepare/commit
  protocol -- the new path is staged before the old path is withdrawn, and
  a staging failure rolls back to the untouched old path rather than
  degrading to current_path=None. Implementing this the naive way (deleting
  by OpenFlow match at commit and rollback) turned out to be unsafe: the
  switch attached to SOURCE and the switch attached to DEST each have one
  flow whose match is identical across every alternate path, so a naive
  delete removed the live, just-staged entry outright rather than leaving
  or restoring it -- confirmed live as a real blackhole in an otherwise
  cleanly-reported repair, fixed by _withdraw_stale_path() and
  _rollback_staged_path(), which skip or restore that colliding match
  instead of deleting it, re-verified against the same live fault injection
  that found the bug. The hold-down window's start moved out of
  decide_link_event() (which set it at decision time, before any I/O ran)
  into main(), gated on execute_repair() reporting an actual commit,
  re-verified live by observing correct suppression of the same physical
  link's other interface reporting the identical fault moments later.
  v0.13.0 closes three further gaps in this same rollback protocol, found
  by a sixth review pass reading the code again after it was already
  tested and live-verified: rollback's own result was previously
  discarded, so a rollback that itself failed partway through was reported
  as a confirmed-intact old path anyway -- fixed with a distinct
  repair_rollback_incomplete event and current_path=None whenever rollback
  does not fully succeed. Rollback recomputed the intended new path from
  scratch instead of acting on what install_path() actually confirmed
  staged, meaning a flow the forwarding-consistency check correctly
  rejected could still be "restored" at a colliding boundary match --
  silently overwriting the very foreign flow that check had just
  protected -- fixed by having install_path() return the real staged list
  and _rollback_staged_flows() (renamed from _rollback_staged_path()) act
  only on it. And staging installed flows in natural SOURCE-to-DEST order
  rather than deferring boundary-hop collisions to last, so a live switch
  could repoint onto the still-incomplete new path before the rest of it
  was ready -- fixed with old_path-aware staging order, non-colliding
  flows first. All three re-verified against the same live fault injection
  that found v0.12.0's original blackhole. Also this round: _agent_cookie()
  widened from a truncated 32-bit value to the full 64-bit OpenFlow
  cookie-field width; and the forwarding-consistency check's documentation
  corrected to state precisely what it guarantees (a conflict present at
  check time is detected and rejected, not an atomic race-free guarantee
  against a concurrent writer -- consistent with this paper's declared
  one-process-per-pair deployment model). Contains pure-logic
  unit tests for the path computation and all hold-down/startup behaviours
  (suppression, stale-state reconciliation, cross-interface coverage,
  edge-confirmation, startup synchronization, link_state validation at both
  startup and runtime, multi-OVS target routing, adapter unification,
  partial-failure honesty, endpoint genericity, cookie-scoped deletion,
  startup-install honesty, repair-retry liveness, pair-scoped-cookie
  determinism, monitor-subprocess reconnect, forwarding-consistency
  rejection, two-phase rollback with boundary-hop-collision handling, the
  rollback protocol's own result-honesty/staged-subset/staging-order
  correctness, and ambiguous-timeout read-back resolution (v0.14.0: a
  seventh review pass found apply_flow() treated a subprocess timeout as
  a confirmed failure identically to a non-zero exit, though a timeout
  only means the client gave up waiting, not that the switch never
  applied the operation -- fixed by reading the switch's actual state
  back directly and resolving True/False only once confirmed, raising
  rather than guessing if even the read-back cannot tell; live-verified
  only as a regression check on the ordinary path, since no measured
  repetition has ever actually timed out; and service-restoration
  decomposition instrumentation (v0.15.0, the first Layer-2 evaluation
  step, deliberately landed before further live experiments so the final
  timing dataset is collected once against fully instrumented code):
  bfs_start_ns/bfs_end_ns bracketing every bfs_path() call site,
  stage_start_ns/stage_end_ns and commit_start_ns/commit_end_ns in
  execute_repair(), all copied into the logged event without changing
  repair_start_ns/repair_end_ns's pre-existing meaning -- live-verified
  against the multi-OVS testbed, confirming an internally consistent
  BFS/stage/commit breakdown on a real fault injection), all
  runnable without Mininet/OVS; live-network flapping-link,
  startup-already-down, and multi-OVS-deployment scripts and their raw data
  and event logs across every pre-fix and post-fix run, retained side by
  side; the network-level Stage 3 raw data (five autonomous link-failure
  repetitions, plus scripted non-autonomous switch/controller-failure
  conditions) and reports; and the figure-generation script and resulting
  figures used in the manuscript.
keywords:
  - software-defined networking
  - self-healing networks
  - event-driven fault detection
  - autonomous path recovery
  - OVSDB
  - OpenFlow
  - hold-down timers
  - control-plane resilience
  - reproducible systems research
